// A body file (§15.1), read by `ucal cal derive`.
//
// Every parameter carries Rule C's obligations — the published value verbatim,
// its unit, a citation, and the half-width of the window it is valid over.
// A file that omits any of them is refused rather than defaulted.
//
// # This example ships as a body, deliberately
//
// It did not, until 1.5.0 added Europa to the catalogue, and the collision was
// worth keeping rather than avoiding: it makes the file checkable. Deriving
// this file must produce the same intercalation as `ucal cal show europa-d`,
// and a test asserts it. An example nobody can check is a claim with no
// mechanism, which is the thing this project refuses everywhere else.
//
// # The solar day is derived, and this is why that matters
//
// It is not a measurement. No source publishes a solar day for a tidally
// locked moon: tidal lock fixes Europa's face towards Jupiter, not towards the
// Sun, so a solar day is the synodic period of the rotation and the year, and
// it follows exactly from two figures that *are* published:
//
//     solar_day = 1 / (1/P_rotation - 1/P_year) = 3.554094092244058... d
//
// `derived: synodic` computes that in ticks, exactly, from the two parameters
// in this file. Until 1.5.0 the format had no way to say it, and the value had
// to be written down — and writing it down means rounding it, which changes the
// calendar. The intercalation rule this program derives from this same file at
// each precision, measured rather than reasoned about:
//
//      2 decimals  3.55            -> 47/105
//      3 decimals  3.554           ->   2/27
//      4 decimals  3.5541          ->  5/126
//      5 decimals  3.55409         ->  5/116
//      6 decimals  3.554094        ->   1/24
//     12 decimals  3.554094092244  ->   1/24
//      derived: synodic            ->   1/24, and no digits to get wrong
//
// Six decimals happen to settle Europa, and that is a fact about Europa rather
// than a rule: its chosen convergent is the second, reached before the far
// terms of the continued fraction start moving. A body whose rule sits deeper
// needs more, and there is no way to know in advance which. That is the
// argument for the derived form — not convenience, but that the right number of
// digits is not knowable before deriving the calendar you were trying to write.
//
// # Rounding is not the only way to get this wrong
//
// An earlier version of this file stated `3.552106` — six decimals, so precision
// was never the problem — and cited the NASA fact sheet for it. That source does
// not publish a solar day for a tidally locked moon at all, and the figure was
// wrong in the third decimal. It derived 202/279 against the shipped body's
// 1/24: a different calendar, in the file that documents how to write one.
//
// It loaded perfectly, and survived a full release cycle, because the only
// check on it was that it loaded. A wrong number with a citation attached is
// worse than a wrong number, and it is invisible until something compares it to
// an independent computation of the same quantity. Y3 shipped `europa-d` and
// the comparison finally existed.

id: europa
primary: jupiter

// Sidereal rotation. Synchronous with the orbit about Jupiter — the fact sheet
// prints `S` in the rotation column — so this figure is both.
rotation_period: {
  value: 3.551181
  unit: d
  citation: NASA Planetary Fact Sheets (Williams, D. R.), Jovian satellite fact sheet; the original document is available in the Internet Archive
  locator: https://web.archive.org/web/2020/https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/joviansatfact.html
  valid_years: 10000
}

// Derived, not measured. A derived parameter states no value and no unit — it
// is computed in ticks from the parameters it names — and still carries a
// citation, because a derived value is not an uncited one: it is cited to the
// derivation and to the figures underneath it, and `ucal cal derive` prints all
// three.
solar_day: {
  derived: synodic
  citation: derived as 1 / (1/P_rotation - 1/P_orbital_period) from the two published figures cited in this file; no source publishes a solar day for a tidally locked moon
  valid_years: 10000
}

// Rule K.2: a satellite's year is its primary's orbit about the Sun, not its
// own about the primary. Its own is the cycle, and Europa has no moon, so it
// has no cycle at all (§15.3 forbids supplying one).
orbital_period: {
  value: 4332.589
  unit: d
  citation: NASA Planetary Fact Sheets (Williams, D. R.), Jupiter fact sheet; the original document is available in the Internet Archive
  locator: https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/
  valid_years: 10000
}
